In article <36d438f7.12866181@news.unilim.fr>, olivierrobert@minitel.net (Olivier ROBERT) writes:
> Is it possible to create a hard link to a directory?
> I need this because I want to grant access to a user in a directory
> which belongs to another user in the same group, but I don't want
> everyone in the group to be able to access to that directory.
> Performing a hard link to that directory in the first user's home
> directory would be a solution...if possible. Can anyone help me?
> Thanks!
> 
> O.R.

No, hard links work only with files.
Soft links work, and the effective result is much comparible.

Neither a hard nor a soft link would fulfill your requirement, though.
(Hard-linked files share one inode that holds the permission and ownership,
if you change permission on one link, the other link(s) would change, too.)

A possible approach is: create another group, make both users members of the
group, and do (or have them do) something with newgrp.

Another approach is: give both users the same UID/GID in passwd. The
drawback is, the user that is FIRST in passwd will always appear as owner,
and some applications (e.g. screenlock) might be confused.

